omly colouring graphs with lower bounds on\\n    girth and maximum degree. (English summary) (see 2003h:68005)\\n    Eisenblätter, Andreas (with Grötschel, Martin; Koster, Arie M. C. A.) Frequency planning\\n    and ramiﬁcations of coloring. (English summary) 2003j:05120\\n    Eppstein, David Improved algorithms for 3-coloring, 3-edge-coloring, and constraint\\n    satisfaction. (English summary) (see 2003i:68002)\\n    Farrell, E. J. A note on matching polynomials and colourability of graphs. (English\\n    summary)\\n    Favaron, Odile see Bacsó, Gábor, 2003m:05141\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nSaieed Akbari - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/author/saieed-akbari)\\nciteturn0search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; We prove the conjecture for special graph families, including line graphs and planar graphs, and provide examples where the conjecture is exact.\\nThe conjecture also relates to a question posed by Torgašev. We prove the conjecture for special graph families, including line graphs and planar graphs, and provide examples where the conjecture is exact. We also conjecture that for any connected graph $G$, its line graph $L(G)$ satisfies $n^+(L(G)) \\\\le n^-(L(G)) + 1$, and obtain partial results.\\n\\ngraph line graph planar graph regular graph strongly regular graph connected graph planar\\n\\n2025-06-08\\n\\nSaieed Akbari, Hitesh Kumar, Bojan Mohar, Shivaramakrishna Pragada, Shengtong Zhang\\n\\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\\n\\nRefinement of a conjecture on positive square energy of graphs\\n\\nLet $G$ be a simple graph of order $n$ with eigenvalues $λ_1(G)\\\\geq \\\\cdots \\\\geq λ_n(G)$. Define \\\\[s^+(G)=\\\\sum_{λ_i >0} λ_i^2(G), \\\\quad s^-(G)=\\\\sum_{λ_i<0} λ_i^2(G).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nouterplanar — arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/keyword/outerplanar?page=14)\\nciteturn0search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; We resolve this conjecture in the negative, and provide a process for generating families of counterexamples. ... However, the answer to Gallai's question is positive for several well-known classes of graphs, as for instance connected outerplanar graphs, connected split graphs, and 2-trees. ... The Fiedler value $λ_2$, also known as algebraic connectivity, is the second smallest Laplacian eigenvalue of a graph. ... We also provide bounds on the maximum Fiedler value for the following classes of planar graphs: Bipartite planar graphs, bipartite planar graphs with minimum vertex degree~3, and outerplanar graphs.\\ngraph outerplanar\\n\\n2012-06-18 v2\\n\\nLali Barrière, Clemens Huemer, Dieter Mitsche, David Orden\\n\\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\\n\\nOn the Fiedler value of large planar graphs\\n\\nPublished in Linear Algebra and its Applications 439:7 (2013), 2070-2084 • View Publication • BIB\\n\\nThe Fiedler value $λ_2$, also known as algebraic connectivity, is the second smallest Laplacian eigenvalue of a graph. We study the maximum Fiedler value among all planar graphs $G$ with $n$ vertices, denoted by $λ_{2\\\\max}$, and we show the bounds $2+Θ(\\\\frac{1}{n^2}) \\\\leq λ_{2\\\\max} \\\\leq 2+O(\\\\frac{1}{n})$. We also provide bounds on the maximum Fiedler value for the following classes of planar graphs: Bipartite planar graphs, bipartite planar graphs with minimum vertex degree~3, and outerplanar graphs. Furthermore, we derive almost tight bounds on $λ_{2\\\\max}$ for two more classes of graphs, those of bounded genus and $K_h$-minor-free graphs.\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nau:Ouyang_Z in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AOuyang_Z+in%3Amath)\\nciteturn0search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; In 2018, Angelini et al. showed that any n-vertex bipartite IC-planar graph has at most 2.25n-4 edges, which implies that bipartite IC-planar graphs have vertex-connectivity at most 4.\\n\\n  * The minimum size of maximal bipartite IC-plane graphs with given connectivity\\n\\nGuiping Wang, Yuanqiu Huang, Zhangdong Ouyang, Licheng Zhang\\n\\nJun 03 2025 math.CO arXiv:2506.00878v1\\n\\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\\n\\n0\\n\\n@misc{2506.00878, author = {Guiping Wang and Yuanqiu Huang and Zhangdong Ouyang and Licheng Zhang}, title = {{T}he minimum size of maximal bipartite {IC}-plane graphs with given connectivity}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2506.00878}, note = {arXiv:2506.00878v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\\n\\nPDF\\n\\nRecently, the problem of establishing bounds on the edge density of 1-planar graphs, including their subclass IC-planar graphs, has received considerable attention. In 2018, Angelini et al. showed that any n-vertex bipartite IC-planar graph has at most 2.25n-4 edges, which implies that bipartite IC-planar graphs have vertex-connectivity at most 4. In this paper, we prove that any n-vertex maximal bipartite IC-plane graph with connectivity 2 has at least 3/2n-2 edges, and those with connectivity 3 has at least 2n-3 edges. All the above lower bounds are tight. For 4-connected maximal bipartite IC-planar graphs, the question of determining a non-trivial lower bound on the size remains open.\\n\\n  * The minimum crossing number and minimum size of maximal 1-plane graphs with given connectivity\\n\\nZhangdong Ouyang, Yuanqiu Huang, Licheng Zhang, Fengming Dong\\n\\nMay 01 2025 math.CO arXiv:2504.21558v1\\n\\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\\n\\n0\\n\\n@misc{2504.21558, author = {Zhangdong Ouyang and Yuanqiu Huang and Licheng Zhang and Fengming Dong}, title = {{T}he minimum crossing number and minimum size of maximal 1-plane graphs with given connectivity}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2504.21558}, note = {arXiv:2504.21558v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\\n\\nPDF\\n\\nA 1-planar graph is a graph which has a drawing on the plane such that each edge is crossed at most once. If a 1-planar graph is drawn in that way, the drawing is called a \\\\it 1-plane graph. A graph is maximal 1-plane (or 1-planar) if no additional edge can be added without violating 1-planarity or simplicity. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nCourses and seminars — Combinatorics, Graph Theory and Applications. COMBGRAF — UPC. Universitat Politècnica de Catalunya (https://combgraph.upc.edu/en/Courses%20and%20seminars)\\nciteturn0search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; The degree/diameter problem in maximal planar bipartite graphs ... We consider the $(\\\\Delta,D)$ problem for maximal planar bipartite graphs, that is, simple planar graphs in which every face is a quadrangle.\\nJoint work with Cristina Dalfó.\\n\\nCOMPUTATIONAL GEOMETRY SEMINAR\\nFriday, December 4, 2015, 12:30-13:30  (15 minutes delayed with respect to the usual schedule)\\nRoom S215 Omega Building, Campus Nord UPC  (equiv.: Room 215 Floor -2)\\nClemens Huemer, Universitat Politècnica de Catalunya\\n\\nThe degree/diameter problem in maximal planar bipartite graphs\\n\\nAbstract:\\n\\nThe $(\\\\Delta,D)$ (degree/diameter) problem consists of finding the largest possible number of vertices $n$ among all the graphs with maximum degree $\\\\Delta$ and diameter $D$. We consider the $(\\\\Delta,D)$ problem for maximal planar bipartite graphs, that is, simple planar graphs in which every face is a quadrangle. We obtain that for the $(\\\\Delta,2)$ problem, the number of vertices is $n=\\\\Delta+2$; and for the $(\\\\Delta,3)$ problem, $n= 3\\\\Delta-1$ if $\\\\Delta$ is odd and $n= 3\\\\Delta-2$ if $\\\\Delta$ is even. Then, we prove that, for the general case of the $(\\\\Delta,D)$ problem, an upper bound on $n$ is approximately $3(2D+1)(\\\\Delta-2)^{\\\\lfloor D/2\\\\rfloor}$, and another one is $C(\\\\Delta-2)^{\\\\lfloor D/2\\\\rfloor}$ if $\\\\Delta\\\\geq D$ and $C$ is a sufficiently large constant. Our upper bounds improve for our kind of graphs the one given by Fello